-- title:
--   Inertia at 2 cannot fix a Hecke eigenplane of J₀(p)
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $B$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ lying over $2$, in the sense that $2$ is a non-unit of $B$. Write $J = \mathrm{JZero}\,p$ for the group of degree-zero divisor classes of the modular function field of level $p$ base changed to $\overline{\mathbf Q}$, equipped with the module structure over $\mathrm{HeckeAlg} = \mathbf Z[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ given by `heckeModuleBar p` (evaluation of polynomials at the Hecke operators on $J$ when these commute, and the trivial action via evaluation at $0$ otherwise), let $T = \mathrm{TateModule}\,2\,J$ be the group of sequences $(x_n)$ in $J$ with $2^n x_n = 0$ and $2x_{n+1} = x_n$, and let $V = \mathbf Q_2 \otimes_{\mathbf Z_2} T$, on which $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbf Q}/\mathbf Q)$ acts by $\rho$, the base change of the termwise Galois action, and on which the $\mathbf Q_2$-subalgebra $A \subseteq \mathrm{End}_{\mathbf Q_2}(V)$ generated by the image of the Hecke action acts. Assume $A$ is finite-dimensional over $\mathbf Q_2$, that $\rho(\sigma)(a v) = a\,\rho(\sigma) v$ for all $\sigma$, $a \in A$, $v \in V$, and that $A$ is commutative. Let $\mathfrak m \subset A$ be a maximal ideal and let $c_0, c_1 \in V$ satisfy: whenever $a_0 c_0 + a_1 c_1 = 0$ with $a_i \in A$ one has $a_0, a_1 \in \mathfrak m$; every element of $\mathfrak m$ kills $c_0$ and $c_1$; every $v \in V$ killed by $\mathfrak m$ is of the form $k_0 c_0 + k_1 c_1$ with $k_i \in A$; for every $\sigma \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbf Q}/\mathbf Q)$ there is a matrix $(a_{ji}) \in A^{2\times 2}$ with $\rho(\sigma) c_j = a_{j0} c_0 + a_{j1} c_1$ such that, for every prime $\ell$ not dividing $2p$ and every valuation subring $A'$ of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ lying over $\ell$ for which $\sigma$ lies in the decomposition subgroup of $A'$ and acts on its residue field by $x \mapsto x^{\ell}$, one has $a_{00}a_{11} - a_{10}a_{01} = \ell$ in $A$; and finally every element of the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbf Q}/\mathbf Q)$ of the inertia subgroup of $B$ fixes both $c_0$ and $c_1$. Then these hypotheses are contradictory: the conclusion is `False`.
--
--   The assertion is that the $\mathfrak m$-eigenplane of the rational $2$-adic Tate module of $J_0(p)$, a two-dimensional space over the residue field $A/\mathfrak m$ on which Frobenius determinants are the primes themselves (so that the determinant character is the cyclotomic character), cannot be fixed pointwise by inertia at $2$, since the cyclotomic character is ramified at $2$. It is the contradiction step used by [`ModularCurve.exists_mem_inertiaSubgroupIn_tateModule_rep_ne_of_adjoin_tateHeckeRep_apply_ne_zero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_mem_inertiaSubgroupIn_tateModule_rep_ne_of_adjoin_tateHeckeRep_apply_ne_zero) to produce an inertia element at $2$ acting non-trivially on the Tate module.
